From 6d2deb01b45c8dea2af41f62807409e8d748772b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Udhayveer Singh <udhayveersingh@Udhayveer.local>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 00:28:45 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] Carry the tunnel across a flow kill instead of rebuilding
 after it

The ISP on the target network sweeps established TCP flows and tears
down every long-lived one at once (measured 2026-09-15: Google, Apple
and Cloudflare TLS flows all stalled in the same second while ICMP kept
flowing). Every mitigation so far has been reactive - PINGs to spot a
black-holed flow, wedge detection, always-reconnect - so the floor
stayed "notice the kill, rebuild, stall".

Three pieces make a kill survivable instead:

  --hot-standby keeps a second MASQUE session handshaken and idle, so a
  lost flow is replaced by a promotion rather than a dial. This is also
  what makes aggressive detection safe: the -k 2s experiment was worse
  than -k 5s because every false positive cost a rebuild, and a warm
  standby reduces that cost to a promotion.

  --flow-ttl retires a healthy flow before it is old enough to be swept,
  jittered so the cadence is not itself a signature. A rotation with no
  warm standby is deferred, never forced.

  A retired flow lingers, still draining into the device, and the packet
  its device pump had already read is carried into the next session
  instead of dropped - the same fault d5068ff fixed for the wake packet.

Measured over the real network, 30s TTL: 11 rotations, zero kills, zero
failed requests, promotion within the same second.

nativetun gains a darwin backend (utun via wireguard/tun); routing is
left to the caller, which owns the original gateway.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
